---
// Case study for paradedb#4924. The error, the code and the review findings are
// quoted or summarised from the issue (#4843), the pull request thread and the
// merged patch.
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Frames from "../../components/cs/Frames.astro";

const ERROR =
  "ERROR:  internal error: entered unreachable code: get_insert_state: tried to get the InsertState for relation 8360077 but it was never pushed";

// Quoted from the merged patch; "// …" marks lines left out
const GUARD = `impl Drop for FrameGuard {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        // Safety: we are on the Postgres main thread, inside an executor hook whose entry pushed
        // one frame, so the stack is non-empty.
        unsafe {
            if std::thread::panicking() {
                // We are already unwinding.  Calling insertcleanup now risks a second panic,
                // which would abort the process.  Leave the frame in place; the xact-abort
                // callback registered above will clear the stack when the transaction rolls back.
                return;
            }

            let frame = EXECUTOR_RUN_STACK
                .pop()
                .expect("FrameGuard::drop: stack underflow — frame was never pushed");

            for (_, mut entry) in frame.active {
                // …
                let mode = std::mem::replace(&mut entry.insert_state.mode, InsertMode::Completed);
                insertcleanup(&entry.insert_state, mode);
            }
        }
    }
}`;

const INVARIANTS = `//! # Stack invariants
//!
//! * \`EXECUTOR_RUN_STACK.len()\` equals the number of live [\`FrameGuard\`] instances. Each guard
//!   pushes exactly one frame on creation and pops exactly one frame on drop.
//!
//! * \`push_insert_state\` always targets the **top** frame. \`aminsert\` is always called from
//!   within the hook invocation that created its frame, so the top of the stack is always the
//!   correct frame.
// …
//! * **What is NOT guaranteed**: the stack is not "all non-empty below all empty". A
//!   \`ProcessUtility\` hook can push a frame on top of an active \`ExecutorRun\` frame. This is
//!   fine — each frame is independent and cleaned up by its own guard.`;
---

<CaseStudy
  title="A panic on PG16, and the fix that needed fixing"
  dek="A multi-row insert panicked inside ParadeDB when an index expression re-entered the executor. My first fix traded that panic for a different one. The merged fix gives every nesting level its own frame."
  pr={{ ref: "#4924", url: "https://github.com/paradedb/paradedb/pull/4924", merged: "17 May 2026", additions: 244, deletions: 46, files: 4 }}
  issue={{ ref: "#4843", url: "https://github.com/paradedb/paradedb/issues/4843" }}
  next={{ title: "Re-reading a crash-safe indexer", href: "/work/evm-indexer" }}
>
  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The report</h2>
    <p>
      A user updating a table of about three million rows got this from a bm25 index that includes an expression,
      <code>bingo.cansmiles("smiles")</code>:
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-term"><span class="is-bad">{ERROR}</span></div>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Why it happened</h2>
    <p>
      Postgres 17 added <code>aminsertcleanup</code>, which lets an index defer work until an insert has finished. On
      PG15 and PG16, ParadeDB approximates it by hooking <code>ExecutorRun</code>, <code>ExecutorFinish</code> and
      <code>ProcessUtility</code>, and keeping a stack of insert states that are still in progress.
    </p>
    <p>
      The run hook pushed a new empty entry for every <code>ExecutorRun</code>. When the index expression started a
      run of its own partway through the insert, that nested run put an empty entry on top of the one holding the
      insert's state.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <Frames
        label="EXECUTOR_RUN_STACK, top first"
        frames={[
          { code: "None", note: "pushed by the nested ExecutorRun", empty: true },
          { code: "Some { index: state }", note: "the insert in progress" },
        ]}
        then="The next lookup reads the top, finds nothing, and hits unreachable!"
        outcome="bad"
      />
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>First fix: count the depth</h2>
    <p>
      If the top entry already held state, a nested run would bump a counter and leave the stack alone, and the
      matching finish would decrement it. Cleanup ran only once the count was back at zero.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>A test that proved nothing</h2>
    <p>
      The real trigger needs a third-party extension, so I tried to reproduce the nesting without it. I wrote a
      function that runs a query through SPI, used it in an expression index, watched the test pass, and said so on
      the pull request.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then I ran the same test against the unfixed code. It passed there too. Standard SPI does not re-enter the
      <code>ExecutorRun</code> hook, so the test had never exercised the bug. I retracted the claim and removed the
      test.
    </p>
    <p>
      The fix was validated by hand on PG16 with the extension installed, and later by the person who reported the
      bug.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>What review found</h2>
    <p>
      A maintainer traced a case the counter broke: a trigger, or a function in an index expression, that inserts
      back into the same indexed table. The original code had handled this, because every run got its own entry. With
      the counter, the nested insert landed in the same map as the outer one.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <Frames
        label="EXECUTOR_RUN_STACK with the depth counter"
        frames={[{ code: "Some { index: outer state }, depth: 1", note: "one entry, shared by both inserts" }]}
        then="The nested insert asks for a slot for the same index, finds it occupied, and hits a different unreachable!"
        outcome="bad"
      />
    </figure>
    <p>I walked the trace on paper and it held. The counter had traded one panic for another on a narrower path.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The rebuild: one frame per invocation</h2>
    <p>
      The merged fix removes the counter. Each hook invocation creates a <code>FrameGuard</code>, which pushes a fresh
      frame when it is created and cleans that frame up when it is dropped. A nested insert gets an empty frame of its
      own, so it cannot collide with the outer one, and cleanup happens on every exit path because it is tied to
      scope.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <Frames
        label="EXECUTOR_RUN_STACK with FrameGuard"
        frames={[
          { code: "InsertFrame { index: inner state }", note: "owned by the nested invocation's guard" },
          { code: "InsertFrame { index: outer state }", note: "owned by the outer invocation's guard" },
        ]}
        then="Each guard cleans up and pops its own frame as it goes out of scope, innermost first."
        outcome="good"
      />
    </figure>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-code"><Code code={GUARD} lang="rust" theme="css-variables" /></div>
      <figcaption>From fake_aminsertcleanup.rs, as merged. "// …" marks lines left out.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The guard does one more thing. If it is dropped while the thread is already unwinding from a panic, it skips
      cleanup, because a second panic at that point would abort the process.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Writing down what holds</h2>
    <p>The module now states its invariants, including the one it does not provide.</p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-code"><Code code={INVARIANTS} lang="rust" theme="css-variables" /></div>
      <figcaption>From the module documentation, as merged. "// …" marks lines left out.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>One more path</h2>
    <p>
      Before merge, review raised <code>INSERT ... RETURNING</code> read through a cursor: several runs against a
      single finish. Cleaning up at the end of each run could leave a cached pointer to state that was already gone,
      so the guard now clears that cache as part of cleanup.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Confirmed</h2>
    <p>
      The person who reported the bug ran the patch against their own setup and confirmed the panic was gone. It
      merged on 17 May 2026, twelve commits after the first version.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
